## 🖥️ CHAPTER 1: What Even IS an Operating System?

Before touching a single line of code, you need to understand the stage you're building on.

When you buy a computer, you have **hardware** — CPU, RAM, hard disk, keyboard, USB ports. These are just physical components. They don't know what "running Chrome" means. That's where the **Operating System (OS)** comes in.

The OS is the **middleman software** between your physical hardware and the applications you run. Its job is:
- Manage who gets to use the CPU (scheduling)
- Manage RAM (memory management)
- Talk to hardware devices (drivers)
- Give applications a safe, consistent way to do things

The **kernel** is the **core part** of the OS — the part that actually does all of this. On Linux, the kernel is literally a file called `vmlinuz` that gets loaded when your computer boots.

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                 YOUR APPLICATIONS                │
│         Chrome, Python, Vim, your test_app       │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│              STANDARD LIBRARY (glibc)            │
│    printf(), malloc(), fopen() — nice wrappers   │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│           SYSTEM CALL INTERFACE                  │
│   The "front door" to the kernel (read, write,   │
│   open, mmap, ioctl...) — ~350 system calls      │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                 LINUX KERNEL                     │
│  ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────────────┐ │
│  │ Process  │ │ Memory   │ │ Device Drivers   │ │
│  │ Scheduler│ │ Manager  │ │ (YOUR CODE HERE) │ │
│  └──────────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────────────┘ │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                   HARDWARE                       │
│      CPU    RAM    Disk    USB    GPU    ...      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

