## 🔐 CHAPTER 2: User Space vs Kernel Space (The Most Important Concept)

This is **the** most critical concept in kernel programming. Get this right and everything else makes sense.

### CPU Privilege Rings

Modern CPUs run in different **privilege levels** (called "rings" on x86):

```
Ring 0 ─── KERNEL MODE  ─── Can do ANYTHING
Ring 1 ─── (rarely used)
Ring 2 ─── (rarely used)
Ring 3 ─── USER MODE    ─── Heavily restricted
```

When the Linux kernel runs, it runs in **Ring 0** — it can directly access memory, send signals to the CPU, talk to hardware registers. If it does something wrong, **the entire system crashes** (kernel panic — the dreaded blue/black screen).

When your Chrome browser runs, it runs in **Ring 3** — it CANNOT:
- Access memory belonging to another process
- Directly read/write to hardware
- Execute privileged CPU instructions

If Chrome tries to access memory it doesn't own, the CPU raises a **fault** and the kernel kills Chrome. **Just Chrome, not the whole system.** This is what makes your OS stable.

### Why Does This Matter for Your Driver?

Your driver code runs in **Ring 0 (kernel space)**. This means:
- One bad pointer dereference = entire system crashes
- No standard C library (`printf` doesn't exist — you use `printk` instead)
- You can't use `malloc` — you use `kmalloc`
- Everything you do affects the entire system

Your test app runs in **Ring 3 (user space)**. It talks to your driver through **system calls** — the kernel's official "safe" interface.

