## 🗺️ CHAPTER 12: The Complete Flow (End-to-End)

When your test app runs `write(fd, "Hello", 5)`, here is EXACTLY what happens:

```
test_app.c:
  write(fd, "Hello", 5)
       │
       ▼
glibc:
  Sets up syscall number (SYS_write = 1) and arguments
  Executes 'syscall' instruction
       │
       ▼ (CPU switches to Ring 0)
       │
Linux Kernel VFS layer:
  Looks up fd → gets struct file *
  Checks file permissions
  Calls file->f_op->write(...)
       │
       ▼
YOUR dev_write() function:
  Receives: filep, user_buf="Hello", count=5
  Bounds check: 5 < 1024 ✓
  copy_from_user(device_buffer, user_buf, 5)
       │
       ├── CPU checks user_buf is valid user address ✓
       ├── Handles potential page fault if memory paged out
       └── Copies 5 bytes: device_buffer = "Hello"
  data_len = 5
  returns 5
       │
       ▼ (CPU switches back to Ring 3)
       │
glibc returns 5 to test_app
test_app continues...
```

